# external_enum_by_short_name resolves a bare call-form reference to a namespaced engine enum, which the game rejects

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found by lane-enum while implementing B071, on master, INDEPENDENT of B071.
Left unfixed deliberately: removing the path is a scope decision with its own
blast radius, and B071 only needed the workspace-wins half.

## Defect

`Axis(1)`, with nothing named `Axis` in the workspace, resolves to the Core
enum `UI::Plot::Axis` via `GlobalScope::external_enum_by_short_name`.

The game rejects exactly that spelling:

    Identifier 'Axis' is not a data type in global namespace

**436 engine enum names are reachable this way.**

## Why this path survived

`resolve_unqualified` was ALREADY fixed for this — B056 — and it deliberately
excludes enums. `external_enum_by_short_name` is the surviving path that still
does it, and it is reached from exactly one place: `checker.rs`, the
Ident-callee arm.

So this is the same defect class B056 closed, at a second call site that the
fix did not reach. That is now the third instance in this batch of a check
existing at one of N sites — B076 (a correct check reached from one of four
initializer positions), B073 (`awaitable` hardcoded in a second path beside
`lookup_funcdef`), and this. Worth stating as a pattern rather than three
coincidences: **when a rule is fixed in a resolver, grep for every other
function that answers the same question, because the codebase reaches these
questions from more places than the ticket names.**

## Evidence it is real and currently the ONLY such delta

lane-enum ran a base-vs-branch sweep over **151 real plugins** in
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins`. Before B071 the sweep showed exactly ONE delta —
this false positive. After B071 it is zero, because B071's workspace-wins guard
happens to mask it wherever a workspace type exists. **The masking is
incidental, not a fix**: the false positive survives for any bare call-form use
of one of the 436 names where the workspace declares nothing of that name.

Same shape as B084, where B062's export rooting masked a crediting defect: a
clean gate is not evidence of a clean rule.

## Work

Decide whether `external_enum_by_short_name` should exclude enums the way
`resolve_unqualified` already does, or whether the Ident-callee arm should stop
consulting it. Either is a real scope decision — 436 names is a wide surface and
the direction of change ADDS diagnostics, which is the direction that creates
false positives if the reasoning is wrong.

## Test plan

Red: `Axis(1)` with an empty workspace must diagnose.

Generalise per CLAUDE.md — the 436 names are not homogeneous: cover a Core enum
nested in a namespace (`UI::Plot::Axis`), a Core enum at global scope, a Nadeo
enum under a class scope, and the same spellings in NON-call position, since
this path is specifically the Ident-CALLEE arm and the value position may
already behave.

Required silences in the same fixture: a genuinely global engine enum used bare
must still resolve; a workspace type of the same short name must still win
(B071's guard); and a correctly qualified `UI::Plot::Axis(1)` must still work.

## GAME PROBE REQUIRED, AND CURRENTLY IMPOSSIBLE

The `Identifier 'Axis' is not a data type in global namespace` verdict above
came from the documented rule in `resolve_unqualified` (B056's measurement), NOT
from a fresh probe. Before anyone changes behaviour over 436 names, re-measure —
and note that **all game probing in this repo is blocked by B097** (RemoteBuild
cannot deliver a `load_plugin` payload at all). Do not treat B056's recorded
verdict as a substitute for measuring this specific spelling in call position;
that is exactly the "cited as evidence for something nobody measured" failure
B086 exists for.

## Related

B056 (fixed the same class in `resolve_unqualified`), B071 (whose guard
incidentally masks this), B076 / B073 (the same one-of-N-call-sites pattern),
B097 (blocks the probe), B086 (why the recorded verdict is not enough).

## CORRECTION — the "GAME PROBE REQUIRED, AND CURRENTLY IMPOSSIBLE" section above is WRONG on its premise (lane-resolver, 2026-08-31)

I wrote that section. Two errors in it, and together they make this ticket read
as indefinitely blocked when it is nearly unblocked.

**1. The verdict IS a fresh probe, not a doc-comment rule.** I said
`Identifier 'Axis' is not a data type in global namespace` "came from the
documented rule in `resolve_unqualified` (B056's measurement), NOT from a fresh
probe." It is a verbatim committed row of
`docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv`, game output from 2026-08-30:

    Axis        REJECTED   Identifier 'Axis' is not a data type in global namespace
    AxisFlags   REJECTED   Identifier 'AxisFlags' is not a data type in global namespace
    Align, Cond, EMenuOrder — same

**2. "436 names" is not an independent count.** It is that corpus:
`awk -F'\t' '$1 !~ /::/ && $2=="REJECTED"'` over it returns exactly 436. So all
436 already carry a verbatim game verdict.

**The chain was corpus -> `resolve_unqualified` doc comment -> ticket, and by
the third hop the origin was lost and a measurement had been recorded as a
rule.** That is the same provenance erosion B080 exists to stop, arriving from
the opposite direction: there, a measurement's CONDITIONS were lost; here, its
STATUS AS A MEASUREMENT was lost. Both end with a reader unable to tell
evidence from reasoning.

## What is genuinely unmeasured: ONE POSITION, not 436 names

b052 is TYPE position (`Axis v;`). b053 is VALUE position and contains no
`Axis` row at all. **Call position (`Axis(1)`) is a third**, and the probes
README's own trap says not to generalise across positions — so the caution was
right in substance and wrong in scale.

The 436 share one mechanism, so closing the gap needs B067-style shape
coverage — a Core enum nested in a namespace, a Core enum at global scope, a
Nadeo enum under a class scope — plus a known-good control. **That is four or
five names, not a campaign.** B097 blocks roughly a five-name probe here.

## A construction argument that narrows it further — REASONING, NOT MEASUREMENT

Presented as an argument deliberately, because recording it as a measurement is
exactly what B086 is for. `Axis(1)` in call position is either a type
conversion or a function call. A conversion requires `Axis` to name a data
type, and the game says in the adjacent position that it does not. Nothing
named `Axis` is a function. So the type-position measurement bounds call
position tightly.

That is how someone could justify acting before B097 clears. It must be written
down AS reasoning, and the probe should still happen afterwards to close it.

## Scope: ONE LANE, not a split

The production change is one or two lines at a single call site
(`checker.rs:3673`, the only caller of `external_enum_by_short_name`,
`global_scope.rs:1785`). The work is entirely evidence, and it is B073-sized.

If it must be split, the honest line is B097, not size:
- **unblocked half**: drive all 436 bare REJECTED rows through the Ident-callee
  arm and record how many the crate currently resolves. That is the real
  blast-radius number, needs no game, and **nobody currently knows whether it is
  436 or a handful.**
- **blocked half**: the behaviour change, gated on the ~5-name call-position probe.

Not recommended — the sweep is the fix's own test, and separating them means
writing the harness twice.

## DO NOT CITE A CLEAN FLEET RUN AS EVIDENCE HERE

B071's workspace-wins guard MASKS this wherever a workspace type of the name
exists, which is why the 151-plugin sweep now shows zero deltas. **The
151-plugin sweep can no longer detect a regression in this rule.** Same shape as
B084, where B062's export rooting masked a crediting defect — and the third time
in one day a clean gate turned out to be measuring something other than the rule
it was trusted for.